[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Which events should actually produce a notification? → Due-date reminders only (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Last big question before I lay out approaches — where a reminder actually appears.

  [1mThe fork:[0m a static page can only reach the user while it's loaded. True "notify me when the app is closed" needs a
  push server, which we've ruled out. So the choice is how far to go within that limit.

  - [1mIn-page only[0m — a reminder banner/list rendered in the page. No permission prompt, no service worker, fully testable
    with a fake clock. Invisible if the tab is backgrounded or closed.
  - [1mOS notifications (Notification API)[0m — desktop notifications that surface over other windows while the tab is open,
    including backgrounded. Costs a permission prompt, and permission is a one-shot decision per origin: if the user
    denies it, you can't re-ask. Still silent when the page is closed.
  - [1mBoth, layered[0m — in-page is the source of truth; an OS notification fires additionally when permission has been
    granted.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m both, layered, with one constraint — the permission prompt fires only when the user explicitly
  turns reminders on, never on page load. A cold permission prompt is the fastest way to get permanently denied, and
  that's not recoverable in-app. Layering also keeps the OS notification a thin adapter over logic that's testable
  without a browser permission.
